# Divergences between AArch64 and POWERarm's CURRENT lowering (current.map) that are
# accepted on purpose. check.sh fails on any unsound test NOT listed here.
#
# All six are IRIW: two writers, two readers that must agree on the order of the
# writes. AArch64 is other-multi-copy-atomic and forbids the readers disagreeing once
# each reader's two loads are ordered at all. Power is not multi-copy-atomic and only
# forbids it when a hwsync sits between the two loads. Here the loads are separated
# by an lwsync only: DMB LD lowers to lwsync, and an LDAR's or LDAPR's trailing
# acquire fence is lwsync.
#
# Why accepted: no C or C++ program can depend on these. C++ forbids IRIW only when all
# four accesses are seq_cst, which on AArch64 is STLR writers and LDAR;LDAR readers
# (SC-IRIW1) -- and that case IS forbidden here, by the second LDAR's leading hwsync.
# Every case below has at least one reader load that is not seq_cst. Only hand-written
# AArch64 assembly relying on multi-copy atomicity could observe them.
#
# Cost of closing them: an hwsync between those loads, i.e. DMB LD -> hwsync and the
# LDAR/LDAPR trailing fence -> hwsync. LDAR alone runs ~17 M times in a 3.9 s V8 run
# (checklist P7), so that would roughly double the dominant fence cost for shapes nothing
# compiled can rely on. Revisit if a real program is ever found to need it.
IRIW000   plain writers, readers LDR; DMB LD; LDR
IRIW008   plain writers, readers LDAR; LDR
IRIW012   plain writers, readers LDAPR; LDR
SC-IRIW2  release writers, readers LDAR; LDR
SC-IRIW3  release writers, readers LDAPR; LDR
SC-IRIW4  release writers, readers LDR; DMB LD; LDR
# --- The CO-R class: coherence-order cumulativity, three tests ---
#
# The R shape with a coherence edge and a strong barrier on the reader:
#
#   P0: STR x,1 ; DMB ST ; W y,1        P1: STR y,2 ; DMB SY ; LDR r,x
#   observed: [y]=2 (P0's write to y lost the coherence race) and r == 0
#
# AArch64 forbids it. AArch64 is other-multi-copy-atomic and its barriers are
# cumulative over coherence order, so if P0's write to y is co-before P1's then
# P0's earlier write to x has propagated everywhere P0's write to y has. Power is
# not multi-copy-atomic and lwsync's cumulativity does not extend over `co`, so
# ppc.cat allows it. Closing it means DMB ST -> hwsync (and the STLR case
# STLR -> ...; sync), i.e. exactly the cost question the trailing-sync note above
# describes, on a different edge.
#
# Why accepted, and why these three are listed together: **the divergence is in
# the DMB ST / STLR mapping, not in anything to do with exclusives.** CO-R1 and
# CO-R2 are hand-built controls with a plain STR as P0's second write and no
# exclusive instruction anywhere, and they are unsound; R011Lx is the same cycle
# with P0's second write supplied by an LDXR..STXR pair, and it is unsound for
# the same reason and no other. The reservation lowering (current.map's four
# LDXR/STXR rules) is sound on all 574 other tests of the exclusive family; it
# only made this shape reachable, because diy7's original family never produced
# a 4-edge cycle with two barrier edges.
#
# As with the IRIW rows: no C or C++ program can depend on it. C++ gives
# coherence-order guarantees only between seq_cst operations, which compile to
# STLR/LDAR pairs whose leading hwsync already forbids this.
CO-R1     control, no exclusive: plain writers, DMB ST | DMB SY reader
CO-R2     control, no exclusive: plain writer + STLR, DMB ST | LDAR reader
R011Lx    the same cycle with the writer's second store coming from LDXR..STXR
